# Website downloads = latest release only

SolidExpress download links on the marketing site must **always** resolve to assets from the **current GitHub latest release** (`/repos/solidexpress/solidexpress/releases/latest`).

## Hard rules

1. **One release for every platform.** macOS, Windows, and Linux buttons must all hydrate from the same `releases/latest` payload. Never mix versions (e.g. macOS from `v0.0.1` while Linux/Windows are `v0.0.3`).
2. **No cross-release fallback.** If the latest release is missing an asset (for example no `.dmg`), do **not** walk older releases to find one. Point that button at the latest release page / mark it unavailable instead.
3. **No hardcoded versioned asset URLs** in HTML (`.../releases/download/v0.0.1/SolidExpress-0.0.1-macos.dmg`). Initial `href`s may be `https://github.com/solidexpress/solidexpress/releases/latest` until JS hydrates.
4. **No ZIP alternate download buttons** on the public download row (DMG / Setup.exe / `.tar.gz` only).
5. **No OS-detection “Detected macOS…” hint** and no “installer-ready builds…” tagline on the download section.

## Correct pattern

```js
fetch("https://api.github.com/repos/solidexpress/solidexpress/releases/latest")
  .then((r) => r.json())
  .then((release) => {
    // pick .dmg / setup.exe / .tar.gz from release.assets ONLY
    // set #dl-version and “all assets” link from release.tag_name / release.html_url
  });
```

## Wrong pattern

```js
// ❌ walks every release and returns the first matching asset from any tag
firstAsset(allReleases, /\.dmg$/)
```

When editing `website/index.html` (canonical marketing source in this repo) or syncing to `solidexpress.github.io`, keep this rule intact.
